A C++ implementation of Booth & Lueker's PQ-Tree algorithm.

Kellog S. Booth, George S. Lueker, Testing for the consecutive ones property,
interval graphs, and graph planarity using PQ-tree algorithms, Journal of
Computer and Systems Sciences, 13(3) (1976) 335-379.

NOTE: For many years this code was known to be buggy on rare inputs. In 2026
three bugs were found with an oracle-checked fuzz test and fixed: two in
TemplateP6 and TemplateQ2 that read stale parent pointers at the root of the
pertinent subtree, and one in TemplateQ2/TemplateQ3 that left those pointers
dangling after merging a Q-node. Since then the fuzz test in fuzztest.cc has
passed under AddressSanitizer on hundreds of thousands of random reductions.
Each fix carries a fixed regression case in fuzztest.cc.

An independent implementation can be found as a library within BiVoC:
https://bioinformatics.cs.vt.edu/~murali/papers/bivoc/

Description of files:
The main file for this library is pqtree.h.  It contains an API that can be
used by client code for dealing with PQ-Trees.  There are two binaries:
pqtest and fuzztest.

pqtest runs the pqtree code for one example set of reductions on a single tree,
printing the state of the tree at every step.  This illustrates how the pqtree
is built.

fuzztest generates random reduction sequences and checks the library against
two independent oracles.  See the comment at the top of fuzztest.cc.

Building:
The build uses CMake (3.16 or newer) and produces a static library, pqtree,
plus the two test binaries:

$ cmake -B build
$ cmake --build build
$ ctest --test-dir build

To build with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer enabled:

$ cmake -B build-asan -DPQTREES_SANITIZE=ON
$ cmake --build build-asan
$ ctest --test-dir build-asan
